{"step_index":12232,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T17:55:29Z","thinking":"Analyzing the `getMonitorYAxisConfig` function, it appears the Y-axis configuration is conditional. Specifically, when `currentMode` is 'ram', a memory-specific configuration is returned. This suggests that the Y-axis display adapts based on the currently selected monitoring mode. The configuration sets minimum and maximum memory values, grid color, and tick formatting, specifically labeling the ticks in GB.\n\n\nThe Y-axis configuration is also conditional based on the `currentCpuScale`.  When `currentMode` is 'cpu' and `currentCpuScale` is '400', the maximum Y-axis value is 400%, otherwise it's 100%. This also affects the tick labels to display the vCPU number. The datasets seem to adjust their display based on a locked point index with a different point radius and background color.\n\n\nThe CPU data calculation varies depending on `currentCpuScale`.  When `currentCpuScale` is '400', the overall CPU usage is calculated from individual core percentages, scaling up the values. The top application CPU is also scaled based on the core number in the '400' setting.\n\nConversely, with `currentCpuScale` as '100', the overall CPU usage is presented on a 0-100% scale.  Top application CPU is then normalized to this 100% scale using a minimum bound or scaled down by the vCPU number. The click handler retrieves the index of a selected chart point, considering both direct element hits and coordinate-based lookups, using canvas dimensions and scales to get accurate time-based index to lock that point. The graph point locking sets a boolean flag and stores the index.\n\n\nThe code updates the graph report based on user interaction, specifically handling point locking. Locking a point displays its data persistently, overriding hover effects. Unlocking resets the lock, enabling hover updates, and displays the latest data point. The `updateGraphReport` function conditionally updates the UI elements based on the locked state and point data.\n\n\nThe code updates the report UI elements based on the graph's selected point. The status indicator, dot appearance, and unlock button visibility are adjusted according to interaction state – hover, touch, or locked point. Time displays full date and time or just time depending on data availability.\n\nCPU usage display dynamically presents the value and label with different scales, adapting the display to either a 400% scale or a 100% scale. RAM display shows the used RAM in GB and percentage. Application-specific data like name, CPU, and RAM are displayed, if available.\n\n\nThe CPU scale setting function updates UI elements to reflect the selected scale, either 100% or 400%.  It also refreshes the chart data and report with the new scale. The chart's click event handler is also registered on the canvas to handle direct clicks and touch events. The Chart.js click handler seems to use the same callback.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"diff -u /home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer/monitor.html /home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer/static/monitor.html | head -n 30","Cwd":"/home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer","RunPersistent":false,"WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking diff between root monitor and static monitor","toolSummary":"Diff monitor.html files"}}]}
