{"step_index":12262,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T17:56:43Z","thinking":"Examining the state variables in `monitor.html` reveals several controls for the graph display, including sorting criteria, time ranges, and CPU scaling. These variables directly influence the rendering of performance data. The `updateGraphReport` function likely uses these state variables to construct the visualized performance metrics.\n\n\nThe `updateGraphReport` function appears responsible for updating the displayed graph report elements based on a given data point's status.  Conditional checks handle scenarios like locked points or touch interactions to manage the display's behavior. The function dynamically updates various HTML elements, modifying their text or styling based on whether the data point is locked or being scrubbed.\n\n\nThe function's conditional logic determines the displayed text and visual cues based on whether a data point is selected via a click or touch event. The visual feedback includes changes to text elements, the dot's background color, and potentially hides or shows a button. Time display utilizes different formats depending on whether a full date is available. CPU metrics are rendered with dual representation, showing both an overall percentage and a 400% scale representation depending on the state.\n\n\nThe code updates the RAM display and application-specific CPU/RAM metrics based on the data point. Conditional logic handles cases where application data is missing, displaying \"—\" or \"0\" values. The `lockGraphPoint` and `unlockGraphPoint` functions modify the `isPointLocked` flag, the `lockedPointIndex`, and refresh the graph report using an update function, indicating data locking.\n\n\nThe code resets the \"unlock point\" button and updates the graph report upon range changes or when CPU scale changes. CPU scaling function updates the active state of scale buttons, alters display text related to CPU scale and redraws the chart. The currently locked point, if it exists, dictates which data point is fed to the report update function.\n\n\nThe code updates the chart range label and fetches historical data on range changes.  The `getDatasetsForCurrentMode` function constructs the datasets for the chart based on the current mode (CPU), CPU scaling, and whether a point is locked.  Conditional logic is used to calculate and assign dataset properties such as label, data, colors, and point styling.  The point styling functions handle the visual highlighting of the locked point and the last data point. Data scaling is also dynamically controlled within the dataset creation.\n\n\nThe `getDatasetsForCurrentMode` function generates datasets for the chart based on the selected mode (CPU, RAM, or specific app CPU).  It uses conditional logic and a ternary operator to handle CPU scaling, ensuring either a 100% or 400% scale is displayed as appropriate. Specific application CPU data is scaled if the application data is selected. The returned datasets define chart properties like labels, data, colors, and styling for the graph visualization.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/home/azureuser/IrakIroan/azure_file_explorer/static/monitor.html","EndLine":240,"StartLine":200,"toolAction":"Viewing chart header and mode row in monitor.html","toolSummary":"Read monitor.html chart controls"}}]}
